          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.069,
            0.477,
            0.086
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari |Grade 9| Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.107,
            0.861,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": "AE = FH (since AB = FG, and E and H are midpoints of AB and FG)."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.134,
            0.368,
            0.154
          ],
          "content": "\\angle C E A = \\angle C H F = 9 0 ^ { \\circ } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_12_equation_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.162,
            0.564,
            0.183
          ],
          "content": "CF = CA, as they are radii of the circle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.19,
            0.564,
            0.21
          ],
          "content": "By the RHS congruence, ∆CEA ≅ ∆CHF."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.216,
            0.885,
            0.26
          ],
          "content": "So, CE = CH. Thus the chords of equal length are equidistant from the centre."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.373,
            0.295,
            0.623,
            0.323
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Exercise Set 5.4"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.342,
            0.884,
            0.381
          ],
          "content": "1. Use the Baudhāyana–Pythagoras theorem to show why Theorem 6 must be true."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.392,
            0.608,
            0.451
          ],
          "content": "2. Consider Fig. 5.15. If CE is perpendicular to AB, CH is perpendicular to GH, and CE = CH, show that AB = GF."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.469,
            0.607,
            0.511
          ],
          "content": "3. Solve the previous question using the Baudhāyana–Pythagoras theorem."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.528,
            0.609,
            0.568
          ],
          "content": "Question 2 in the exercise above establishes the following result."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.619,
            0.38,
            0.878,
            0.572
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_12_image_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.572,
            0.607,
            0.614
          ],
          "content": "**Theorem 7:** Chords of a circle that are equidistant from the centre have equal length."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.723,
            0.577,
            0.801,
            0.595
          ],
          "content": "Fig. 5.15"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.635,
            0.849,
            0.678
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "5.6.1 Which of the two unequal chords is farther from the centre?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.686,
            0.884,
            0.727
          ],
          "content": "You have two chords on a circle. One is longer than the other. Which chord is closer to the centre? Can you guess?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.731,
            0.885,
            0.791
          ],
          "content": "**Activity:** Draw a circle. Draw chords of various lengths. Drop a perpendicular to each chord from the centre. Record the length of the chord and its distance from the centre in a table."
        },
        {
